WEBVTT

00:00:04.209 --> 00:00:07.021
Welcome to The Lighter Side of the Spectrum, the podcast

00:00:07.021 --> 00:00:10.042
that celebrates the multiple hats we wear and the juggling

00:00:10.138 --> 00:00:12.966
acts we perform in life. Whether you're parenting kids and

00:00:13.046 --> 00:00:17.272
teens on the autism spectrum, navigating the ever-evolving healthcare industry,

00:00:17.545 --> 00:00:20.566
or building a more inclusive and impactful world, this is

00:00:20.598 --> 00:00:25.049
your space to find connection, inspiration, and growth. Together, we'll

00:00:25.097 --> 00:00:29.519
share stories, tackle challenges, and explore how innovation and inclusion

00:00:29.698 --> 00:00:32.865
create better outcomes for everyone. Because no matter the spectrum,

00:00:33.370 --> 00:00:36.715
there's always a lighter side. Now here's your host, David

00:00:36.748 --> 00:00:37.139
Kaufer.

00:00:41.830 --> 00:00:44.736
Hello again, everybody, and welcome back to The Lighter Side

00:00:44.768 --> 00:00:45.463
of the Spectrum.

00:00:46.528 --> 00:00:49.580
What if a person understands exactly what you are asking,

00:00:50.097 --> 00:00:52.886
but their body cannot reliably carry out the response?

00:00:53.873 --> 00:00:57.084
And what if it looks like refusal is actually a

00:00:57.084 --> 00:00:58.369
motor planning problem?

00:00:59.493 --> 00:01:02.335
And what if that gets labeled as lack of comprehension?

00:01:02.704 --> 00:01:06.349
You know, it's really a disconnect between intention and action.

00:01:07.264 --> 00:01:11.872
For many nonspeaking and unreliably speaking autistic people, communication

00:01:11.936 --> 00:01:14.730
cannot be understood separately from movement.

00:01:15.549 --> 00:01:17.604
The body is part of the conversation.

00:01:19.338 --> 00:01:21.245
So welcome back to The Lighter Side of the Spectrum,

00:01:21.405 --> 00:01:24.754
where we talk honestly about autism, communication, parenting, and what

00:01:24.754 --> 00:01:27.078
becomes possible when we presume competence.

00:01:27.815 --> 00:01:31.068
My guest today, and I'm super excited about this, is

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:31.581
Dr.

00:01:32.302 --> 00:01:32.783
Antigone

00:01:33.824 --> 00:01:34.673
Oriopoulos,

00:01:35.587 --> 00:01:35.667
a

00:01:36.629 --> 00:01:41.019
physiotherapist, researcher, disability advocate, inclusion specialist,

00:01:41.035 --> 00:01:46.758
consultant, and coach based in Edmonton, Alberta. Antigone has dedicated

00:01:46.806 --> 00:01:49.582
much of her work to increasing awareness of the sensory

00:01:49.614 --> 00:01:50.544
motor challenges

00:01:51.363 --> 00:01:55.518
experienced by people without reliable speech. She advocates for earlier

00:01:55.534 --> 00:01:57.042
and ongoing physiotherapy,

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:01.791
greater recognition of motor planning differences, and access to motor-based

00:02:01.791 --> 00:02:03.043
communication for people

00:02:03.813 --> 00:02:05.786
with neurodevelopmental disabilities.

00:02:06.765 --> 00:02:10.439
She also brings a very unique combination of clinical knowledge

00:02:11.177 --> 00:02:15.032
research expertise and practical experience supporting movement through

00:02:15.032 --> 00:02:18.068
activities including gymnastics and swimming.

00:02:18.968 --> 00:02:22.276
And she's also a mom. Antigone, welcome to The Lighter

00:02:22.276 --> 00:02:23.256
Side of the Spectrum.

00:02:24.123 --> 00:02:26.645
Thank you so much. I'm very excited to be here

00:02:26.934 --> 00:02:29.504
and have a conversation with you. Yeah, yeah, we actually

00:02:29.504 --> 00:02:32.074
get to have a conversation, um, as is often the

00:02:32.074 --> 00:02:34.869
case here with The Lighter Side of the Spectrum and

00:02:34.869 --> 00:02:37.968
some of these great guests that I'm able To connect

00:02:38.081 --> 00:02:40.858
with, you know, we've been able to connect, share information,

00:02:42.014 --> 00:02:43.508
share a passion, right, for

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:47.281
this community and our work to support

00:02:48.212 --> 00:02:51.166
motor-based communication. But why don't you start giving us a

00:02:51.166 --> 00:02:52.483
little bit more of your background,

00:02:53.333 --> 00:02:55.292
maybe even more as a mom, and kind of what

00:02:55.340 --> 00:02:58.166
brought you into this field and kind of your lived

00:02:58.166 --> 00:02:58.808
experience?

00:03:00.012 --> 00:03:00.366
Sure.

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:04.267
So my first degree was in physiotherapy. I have a

00:03:04.267 --> 00:03:05.327
BSc PT.

00:03:06.206 --> 00:03:06.594
And

00:03:07.743 --> 00:03:08.180
then I,

00:03:09.847 --> 00:03:14.897
I practiced 1 year on cardiovascular surgery and cardiology, and

00:03:14.897 --> 00:03:16.432
I wanted to try my hands at research. So I

00:03:16.432 --> 00:03:17.190
did my master's.

00:03:18.109 --> 00:03:20.980
And then at that point, I realized I enjoyed doing

00:03:21.012 --> 00:03:23.060
research more than I missed being a clinician. So I

00:03:23.109 --> 00:03:25.966
went to do my PhD in public health. And at

00:03:25.982 --> 00:03:26.755
that point, I

00:03:27.512 --> 00:03:29.478
made the decision actually to let my license go.

00:03:30.332 --> 00:03:32.539
I thought that I would never need it or want

00:03:32.539 --> 00:03:34.712
it again. And it was— Hmm. Not an easy decision

00:03:34.809 --> 00:03:36.760
because I knew there was no coming back to it.

00:03:36.825 --> 00:03:37.002
Mm-hmm.

00:03:37.937 --> 00:03:41.178
And then I had a child with nonspeaking autism.

00:03:41.969 --> 00:03:42.437
And,

00:03:44.069 --> 00:03:44.845
and then when I

00:03:45.863 --> 00:03:47.043
became more educated on his

00:03:47.802 --> 00:03:49.870
condition and realized that his

00:03:51.334 --> 00:03:55.067
primary challenge was his sensory motor impairment, it occurred to

00:03:55.067 --> 00:03:57.592
me that there's, there's this whole population that really

00:03:59.399 --> 00:04:02.219
needs and could benefit from the scope of physiotherapy. So

00:04:03.295 --> 00:04:05.286
Lucky for me, one of my very young classmates had

00:04:05.286 --> 00:04:06.860
developed a bridging program for

00:04:07.952 --> 00:04:11.212
Canadian grads who had moved in out of practice or

00:04:11.308 --> 00:04:15.966
international grads preparing for their national exam. There's this program

00:04:15.982 --> 00:04:17.395
that I could take for 6 months to get my

00:04:17.427 --> 00:04:21.233
license back, and I— Oh, okay. Yeah. So I, I

00:04:21.426 --> 00:04:23.755
had no idea what I was gonna do with my

00:04:23.755 --> 00:04:25.168
license, 'cause at that point I,

00:04:25.923 --> 00:04:25.971
um,

00:04:26.903 --> 00:04:29.119
I, I gave up my career in public health.

00:04:30.018 --> 00:04:33.054
I, I thought, I thought that that was,

00:04:34.291 --> 00:04:36.186
that was it. I'm going to just, I'm going to

00:04:36.202 --> 00:04:38.258
be a good caregiver and a good homeschool teacher for

00:04:38.258 --> 00:04:38.917
my son. And,

00:04:39.816 --> 00:04:42.049
and, but I got my license back and

00:04:42.788 --> 00:04:47.157
then just slowly I found and I made opportunities to,

00:04:48.361 --> 00:04:48.683
to

00:04:49.454 --> 00:04:52.232
get back into practice. And I'm, I'm so thankful I

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:54.545
get to do that. I feel very lucky that I

00:04:54.609 --> 00:04:54.915
get to. Yeah.

00:05:08.955 --> 00:05:11.683
Okay. I was gonna ask about that. Okay. Yeah. And

00:05:11.683 --> 00:05:14.716
he uses a letterboard to communicate and we found

00:05:16.015 --> 00:05:19.738
the letterboard for him. I stumbled upon it like most

00:05:19.738 --> 00:05:22.209
of us do by accident when he was 4 and

00:05:22.209 --> 00:05:22.498
3/4.

00:05:23.716 --> 00:05:26.676
Okay, so pretty, pretty young at least. I mean, that's

00:05:26.676 --> 00:05:29.604
pretty young. That's cool. Yeah. Yeah. And,

00:05:30.897 --> 00:05:32.628
you know, of course, I had to unlearn everything I

00:05:32.773 --> 00:05:35.631
thought about, about what I knew about him, including what

00:05:35.663 --> 00:05:37.180
they told me, which was

00:05:38.020 --> 00:05:39.117
that he had

00:05:40.021 --> 00:05:44.572
little to no receptive language and was severely cognitively delayed.

00:05:45.928 --> 00:05:46.751
And, um,

00:05:48.332 --> 00:05:48.558
and then

00:05:49.887 --> 00:05:50.224
Um,

00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:54.039
yeah, I had to unlearn all that. And yeah, you

00:05:54.071 --> 00:05:57.453
do, you deprogram and kind of reprogram. So how old

00:05:57.453 --> 00:06:00.772
was he? Better. Yeah, right. How old was he when

00:06:00.868 --> 00:06:01.653
he got diagnosed?

00:06:02.791 --> 00:06:02.968
3.

00:06:03.753 --> 00:06:05.917
Okay. 3 years old. Yeah.

00:06:06.751 --> 00:06:08.562
So then, you know, a little bit of a gap.

00:06:09.524 --> 00:06:11.576
Let's— I want to jump into kind of like when

00:06:11.608 --> 00:06:14.349
the body doesn't cooperate in that world, because again, I

00:06:14.349 --> 00:06:17.683
think, you know, to your credit, you recognized that early

00:06:17.716 --> 00:06:20.264
on with your son, um, earlier than a lot of

00:06:20.296 --> 00:06:22.348
us did, right? And again, like you said, know better,

00:06:22.428 --> 00:06:22.812
do better.

00:06:23.549 --> 00:06:27.972
How do you explain the disconnect between intention and movement?

00:06:29.414 --> 00:06:31.802
You know, for people who may not understand, you know,

00:06:31.818 --> 00:06:35.743
again, for a neurotypical person, it seems totally natural, right?

00:06:37.025 --> 00:06:42.393
Right. I think that it really doesn't help that the

00:06:42.393 --> 00:06:46.223
DSM, which is what North America uses for the definition

00:06:46.223 --> 00:06:50.169
of autism and diagnostic criteria, It's really not helping us

00:06:50.169 --> 00:06:51.373
that, that, um,

00:06:52.577 --> 00:06:54.632
yeah, the, the language they use is, is really

00:06:55.467 --> 00:06:58.631
based on subjective observation, and they focus on

00:06:59.465 --> 00:07:02.982
deficits in social communication and social interaction. And they use

00:07:02.998 --> 00:07:06.755
language that really implies deliberate intent behind these

00:07:07.992 --> 00:07:08.602
behaviors.

00:07:09.340 --> 00:07:12.776
And it's really shaped how society has—

00:07:14.092 --> 00:07:16.849
viewed autism. And so we're faced with this

00:07:17.683 --> 00:07:22.188
just pervasive perspective that is so widespread, it's very difficult

00:07:22.188 --> 00:07:23.710
to change. And

00:07:24.977 --> 00:07:27.942
it's frustrating and sad because when we compare it to

00:07:27.942 --> 00:07:28.231
other

00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:34.066
neurological conditions like Parkinson's, Tourette's, catatonia, right? Like,

00:07:34.611 --> 00:07:37.641
when we see someone with Parkinson's struggling, they're struggling to

00:07:37.641 --> 00:07:39.644
move their body, we don't say, oh, we need to

00:07:39.644 --> 00:07:40.750
reduce their behaviors.

00:07:41.889 --> 00:07:44.822
Right. We cut them slack and we understand that they're

00:07:44.870 --> 00:07:48.052
trying to organize their body. And we look at it

00:07:48.117 --> 00:07:48.700
through a motor

00:07:50.367 --> 00:07:53.621
challenge lens, not a social lens, which most of the

00:07:53.686 --> 00:07:54.900
world looks through autism.

00:07:58.280 --> 00:08:00.320
That's a really interesting point. Sorry, I just want to

00:08:00.320 --> 00:08:01.744
jump in because you're right. I think

00:08:02.845 --> 00:08:04.545
that so dominates the

00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:07.540
conversations so often, right?

00:08:08.429 --> 00:08:11.485
Behavior, right? And we'll get into this some more. It's

00:08:11.485 --> 00:08:13.814
such a negative word. It's only a negative word in

00:08:13.991 --> 00:08:14.315
autism.

00:08:15.503 --> 00:08:18.613
Right. And outside of that, that sphere, it's, it's sort

00:08:18.613 --> 00:08:21.290
of a— Kind of neutral, right? Just kind of like—

00:08:21.290 --> 00:08:24.304
It's neutral. Yeah. But with autism, it's, it carries a

00:08:24.304 --> 00:08:26.212
lot of negative connotation.

00:08:27.430 --> 00:08:28.632
And, uh, there is,

00:08:30.027 --> 00:08:34.500
so the latest evidence shows that the sensory motor impairment

00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:38.956
causally explains the differences that we see in social communication

00:08:38.988 --> 00:08:40.399
and, and social interaction.

00:08:42.020 --> 00:08:42.469
And there's,

00:08:43.240 --> 00:08:46.080
there's a number of us researchers who are advocating for

00:08:47.011 --> 00:08:49.242
motor impairment to be added to the DSM as a

00:08:49.242 --> 00:08:53.351
core descriptor, but it's not yet. And so when,

00:08:54.475 --> 00:08:57.203
when I— I mean, that's great. It should be.

00:08:58.166 --> 00:09:01.521
Yeah, that's a really great point. That absolutely should be

00:09:01.521 --> 00:09:03.816
because that's— and I'm sorry to cut you off there.

00:09:03.864 --> 00:09:05.421
But again, I just, I go back to this.

00:09:07.155 --> 00:09:10.208
It's such a core area when you're talking about this,

00:09:10.369 --> 00:09:14.097
particularly again, the nonspeaking autistic population. But it's not even—

00:09:14.466 --> 00:09:17.198
it's not even on most people's radar. Exactly,

00:09:18.001 --> 00:09:20.090
exactly. It's not even on the radar. Like, we are

00:09:20.090 --> 00:09:22.758
so in the dark, and, you know, until you stumble

00:09:22.758 --> 00:09:26.759
upon it. But the research is not that new. Like,

00:09:27.225 --> 00:09:28.494
the research that's showing

00:09:29.635 --> 00:09:29.828
the

00:09:30.712 --> 00:09:31.869
motor impairment is

00:09:33.586 --> 00:09:35.334
I don't know. Not— it's not—

00:09:36.152 --> 00:09:38.879
goes back, well, probably earlier, but like the bulk of

00:09:38.879 --> 00:09:41.510
it, 10 or 15 years. So we're not— So it's

00:09:41.510 --> 00:09:41.638
there.

00:09:42.392 --> 00:09:45.456
It's there. It's this, this— the knowledge translation gap or

00:09:45.456 --> 00:09:46.627
the knowledge to action gap is

00:09:47.526 --> 00:09:49.114
very wide and very pervasive.

00:09:50.253 --> 00:09:53.060
When I, when I do explain to parents, um, client

00:09:53.060 --> 00:09:53.926
parents and colleagues

00:09:55.161 --> 00:09:58.017
that disconnect, um, because that's one of the first things

00:09:58.113 --> 00:09:59.942
I try to tell them, I don't use my own

00:10:00.038 --> 00:10:01.771
words because I know what that feels like. But I

00:10:01.900 --> 00:10:05.141
do have this, a, a video and a transcript of

00:10:05.591 --> 00:10:08.880
2 of our local spellers here. They're, they're young adults,

00:10:09.442 --> 00:10:11.175
Peyton Thorpe and Marcus Hulette.

00:10:12.090 --> 00:10:13.710
And, and they, um,

00:10:14.737 --> 00:10:16.663
I share their words because no matter how well I

00:10:16.663 --> 00:10:19.102
explain it, I, my words will never land as well

00:10:19.150 --> 00:10:21.445
as theirs. So I can read you these 2 paragraphs.

00:10:21.942 --> 00:10:23.371
I would love it. Yeah, I was gonna say, yeah,

00:10:23.403 --> 00:10:25.649
let's, let's hear their words. They're both about, uh, they

00:10:25.649 --> 00:10:27.974
were, uh, Book 21 when they wrote this. This is

00:10:27.974 --> 00:10:28.535
about a year ago.

00:10:29.593 --> 00:10:32.831
So Peyton says, my body often feels like it has

00:10:32.863 --> 00:10:35.412
a mind of its own. It's a constant battle to

00:10:35.412 --> 00:10:37.896
get my limbs to cooperate. I have to find where

00:10:37.896 --> 00:10:41.086
I am in space, fight motor loops and repetitive stims,

00:10:41.279 --> 00:10:43.811
and try to make my motions smooth. It's like I'm

00:10:43.907 --> 00:10:46.104
running a race, but my brain is watching from the

00:10:46.104 --> 00:10:49.181
stands and my body is spinning in circles and running

00:10:49.213 --> 00:10:52.868
backwards. My brain understands, but it's like my body is

00:10:52.900 --> 00:10:57.389
daydreaming. And Marcus responds with, my daydreaming body is the

00:10:57.389 --> 00:11:00.179
perfect analogy. My body is like a puppy that just

00:11:00.179 --> 00:11:02.585
watched a rabbit run by. It knows what it should

00:11:02.585 --> 00:11:05.744
do, but the pull of other things makes it almost

00:11:05.792 --> 00:11:06.385
impossible.

00:11:07.171 --> 00:11:09.624
Imagine wanting to pick up a glass of water, but

00:11:09.800 --> 00:11:11.724
when you go to reach for it, your hand isn't

00:11:11.741 --> 00:11:14.579
there. So you find your hand flapping by your head

00:11:15.108 --> 00:11:17.160
and you can't get it to move. And just as

00:11:17.176 --> 00:11:20.063
your frustration kicks in, your hand starts to move and

00:11:20.063 --> 00:11:21.217
you knock the cup over. Mm-hmm.

00:11:21.925 --> 00:11:26.563
And then Peyton adds, struggle, frustration, jerky movements repeated. The

00:11:26.579 --> 00:11:28.609
despised mantra of my existence.

00:11:30.630 --> 00:11:31.632
Wow. Yeah.

00:11:33.233 --> 00:11:35.028
Yeah. Yeah. I mean, that's just so—

00:11:35.755 --> 00:11:39.280
wow, that's really powerful and just so descriptive. And

00:11:40.604 --> 00:11:43.334
yeah, I mean, I'm kind of speechless because, you know,

00:11:43.673 --> 00:11:46.436
you hear different variations of that. Stone, you know, my

00:11:46.436 --> 00:11:48.516
son Stone has shared, you know,

00:11:49.338 --> 00:11:54.281
His, his variations. And the one consistent part is that

00:11:54.634 --> 00:11:56.977
talking about the disconnect between the brain and the body—

00:11:56.977 --> 00:12:00.122
my body's doing things my brain doesn't want it to

00:12:00.171 --> 00:12:02.995
do, or, you know, my body won't do what my

00:12:02.995 --> 00:12:05.916
brain wants it to do. And I just can't imagine

00:12:07.329 --> 00:12:08.227
the frustration. No.

00:12:08.933 --> 00:12:10.603
I just can't. Like, again, I know, like, in a

00:12:10.619 --> 00:12:11.341
limited way—

00:12:12.448 --> 00:12:15.387
I mean, very limited, not even comparable— but like You

00:12:15.435 --> 00:12:17.850
know, when I, I suck at golf, okay? Like, I

00:12:17.850 --> 00:12:18.591
try to play golf

00:12:20.104 --> 00:12:21.392
and I know how frustrated I get just when I

00:12:21.392 --> 00:12:23.549
can't hit the golf ball, right? You feel like you

00:12:23.549 --> 00:12:26.045
should. Here's the, the ball sitting there, you swing, it

00:12:26.045 --> 00:12:28.798
should be really simple. You don't. You get mad at

00:12:28.798 --> 00:12:31.117
that. And that's just a stupid game. Now imagine just

00:12:31.117 --> 00:12:34.096
trying to function, like, function every day. This is what

00:12:34.112 --> 00:12:34.854
they are,

00:12:35.563 --> 00:12:38.850
are fighting with every, every day. And one thing I

00:12:38.850 --> 00:12:39.269
learned in a

00:12:40.156 --> 00:12:42.122
parent course, um, a few years back in

00:12:42.918 --> 00:12:44.025
Of course, from Philadelphia.

00:12:44.971 --> 00:12:47.152
I love that they, they said,

00:12:48.178 --> 00:12:51.561
um, so if we're talking about brain function, so that

00:12:51.561 --> 00:12:56.676
being auditory processing, visual processing, gross motor function, fine motor,

00:12:56.756 --> 00:12:58.296
our, our ability to talk,

00:12:59.161 --> 00:13:01.294
um, if we call, call that brain function,

00:13:02.048 --> 00:13:05.992
there is no correlation between brain function and intelligence. People

00:13:05.992 --> 00:13:08.750
confuse the two all the time, but there is high

00:13:08.942 --> 00:13:10.129
correlation between

00:13:11.027 --> 00:13:13.963
problems in brain function or brain injury, you could say,

00:13:14.477 --> 00:13:16.899
and the ability to express that intelligence.

00:13:18.086 --> 00:13:18.632
Absolutely.

00:13:20.028 --> 00:13:20.557
Absolutely.

00:13:21.311 --> 00:13:25.081
And again, that's something that you see consistently across the

00:13:25.081 --> 00:13:25.835
population.

00:13:26.750 --> 00:13:29.381
Now, you know, with behavior, and again, kind of talking

00:13:29.397 --> 00:13:32.044
about intent, one of the things you— when you mentioned

00:13:32.044 --> 00:13:34.691
that, it just brought back memories of something else that

00:13:34.691 --> 00:13:35.959
Stone had talked about, which is

00:13:36.713 --> 00:13:39.202
When we had our foray into ABA.

00:13:40.342 --> 00:13:43.087
So, um, as many parents did, right? Uh, and I

00:13:43.087 --> 00:13:44.452
don't know if, if

00:13:45.367 --> 00:13:48.529
you probably were able to avoid it given— Yeah, almost.

00:13:48.594 --> 00:13:50.488
We had like 6 months. You're right, I, I did

00:13:50.905 --> 00:13:52.848
for the most part avoid it. Just about 6 months

00:13:52.848 --> 00:13:53.137
I had it.

00:13:54.177 --> 00:13:55.658
Oh, okay. So you, so you were at— you at

00:13:55.658 --> 00:13:58.965
least had some experience with it, and, and it's just—

00:13:59.013 --> 00:14:02.176
yeah. And because I'm just curious, so, so why did

00:14:02.176 --> 00:14:05.054
you do it? Because the social worker told me to.

00:14:05.263 --> 00:14:07.793
Right. Okay. That's, yeah, that, I was gonna say, that's

00:14:07.858 --> 00:14:10.484
the answer, right? The gatekeepers of this, of the funds

00:14:10.500 --> 00:14:11.177
and the services,

00:14:12.130 --> 00:14:14.538
they're, they come at, they, for level 3 autism, it's

00:14:14.652 --> 00:14:19.809
the triad of oral speech therapy, behavioral therapy, and occupational

00:14:19.841 --> 00:14:21.522
therapy. And what are you supposed to do when you

00:14:21.522 --> 00:14:22.312
don't know any better?

00:14:23.809 --> 00:14:25.659
You do it. And all this. And, and the thing

00:14:25.773 --> 00:14:30.066
is, the research does not support Good quality functional outcomes

00:14:30.162 --> 00:14:33.903
with that for, for nonspeaking autism, especially for communication. It's

00:14:33.952 --> 00:14:36.810
not supported yet. They keep like a lot of money.

00:14:36.986 --> 00:14:39.395
I'm a provider. I'm not just a mom, but like

00:14:39.395 --> 00:14:42.959
I, I, I get government contracts and there's a lot

00:14:42.959 --> 00:14:47.134
of money being thrown at ineffective services and it's

00:14:48.081 --> 00:14:50.441
tragic. That's time taken away from a, like a,

00:14:51.437 --> 00:14:55.807
um, a child's childhood. Yeah. And, and of course, could

00:14:55.807 --> 00:14:56.343
be directed,

00:14:57.333 --> 00:14:58.356
um, for more

00:14:59.379 --> 00:15:00.110
effective therapy.

00:15:01.587 --> 00:15:03.954
Yeah, no, you're absolutely right. And like I said, so

00:15:04.244 --> 00:15:05.999
I think about that, and I remember, like I said,

00:15:06.015 --> 00:15:08.253
we didn't do it for a super long period. It

00:15:08.253 --> 00:15:10.250
was a couple years maybe. I mean, you know, too

00:15:10.298 --> 00:15:13.157
long for our purposes because again, it really didn't address

00:15:14.564 --> 00:15:14.935
anything

00:15:15.857 --> 00:15:18.556
for us. I mean, our goal, like so many other

00:15:18.621 --> 00:15:20.221
parents for so long, was just, we just, you know,

00:15:20.238 --> 00:15:23.394
we want help our child communicate. I mean, that's what

00:15:23.394 --> 00:15:25.993
the goal was, and did nothing for that. But Stone

00:15:26.025 --> 00:15:27.822
has since been able to tell me about how

00:15:28.736 --> 00:15:31.479
he would dread these appointments because he knew that he,

00:15:31.607 --> 00:15:33.115
you know, because they, they do these little

00:15:34.142 --> 00:15:37.174
score sheets, right? And, you know, the compliance. And he,

00:15:37.318 --> 00:15:39.131
and he talks about how, you know, he hated how

00:15:39.147 --> 00:15:42.162
it'd be called non-compliant when he couldn't do— physically couldn't

00:15:42.226 --> 00:15:43.991
do something, right, that they were trying to get him

00:15:43.991 --> 00:15:44.328
to do. Yeah.

00:15:45.338 --> 00:15:48.049
And there was this whole, um, you know, reward and

00:15:48.082 --> 00:15:51.274
punishment structure. And, you know, kind of like, oh, well,

00:15:51.290 --> 00:15:53.972
if he doesn't score in this percentile, then

00:15:54.743 --> 00:15:57.057
you need to take away something. In his case, it

00:15:57.057 --> 00:15:59.403
was, um, a cartoon that he loved to watch called

00:15:59.403 --> 00:16:01.203
the Little Einsteins. I don't know if it's

00:16:01.990 --> 00:16:05.413
still, still a favorite, but anyway, he, he said he,

00:16:05.895 --> 00:16:08.241
he knew that whenever he had ABA, it means that

00:16:08.241 --> 00:16:10.057
he wouldn't be able to watch Little Einsteins.

00:16:11.037 --> 00:16:14.106
Oh, that's so sad. Isn't it? Like, like, that's how,

00:16:14.187 --> 00:16:16.018
you know, that was the association he had with it.

00:16:16.179 --> 00:16:18.708
So anyway, I mean, that's just It just shows how

00:16:20.794 --> 00:16:23.025
backwards it is in terms of, of,

00:16:24.981 --> 00:16:28.757
you know, the approach. And the approach is backwards because

00:16:28.789 --> 00:16:30.961
they were the— especially the ABA, they're trying to

00:16:33.036 --> 00:16:37.214
control and change behavior, but that— it requires

00:16:38.135 --> 00:16:41.092
volitional control over your motor system. That's like a top-down

00:16:41.771 --> 00:16:43.759
approach. But if you don't have the foundation,

00:16:44.576 --> 00:16:48.247
of autonomous motor control, you're going to go— it's going

00:16:48.279 --> 00:16:51.245
to do nothing, do nothing except cause trauma.

00:16:52.607 --> 00:16:54.787
And there's no behavior really, when you think about it,

00:16:54.835 --> 00:16:56.679
that needs to be changed. I mean, at least in

00:16:56.679 --> 00:16:58.458
our case. And I know there are some cases of

00:16:58.458 --> 00:17:01.296
people who have some pretty severe

00:17:02.049 --> 00:17:05.143
challenges. And again, not to say it was 100% worthless,

00:17:05.191 --> 00:17:07.980
and I don't mean to spend our time bashing on

00:17:07.980 --> 00:17:11.859
ABA, but just think about the different approaches between, like

00:17:11.859 --> 00:17:14.761
I said, just thinking about behavior versus just understanding that

00:17:14.809 --> 00:17:17.871
brain-body disconnect. So we'll kind of use that as a

00:17:17.999 --> 00:17:20.195
bridge, right? Like, we'll talk about,

00:17:21.045 --> 00:17:23.882
you know, physiotherapy. Okay. Why don't you introduce us to

00:17:23.931 --> 00:17:27.602
that and how that helps and what the difference is

00:17:27.698 --> 00:17:27.826
there?

00:17:28.836 --> 00:17:29.445
Sure. So,

00:17:30.680 --> 00:17:33.212
so right now, the motor impairment in autism is

00:17:34.271 --> 00:17:37.140
considered a dual diagnosis of

00:17:38.198 --> 00:17:40.507
autism plus developmental coordination disorder.

00:17:42.545 --> 00:17:42.625
The

00:17:43.511 --> 00:17:47.214
largest study from the SPARK study shows that— I think

00:17:47.214 --> 00:17:48.067
the statistic is

00:17:49.452 --> 00:17:50.160
at least 90%

00:17:50.901 --> 00:17:53.094
of people with autism, and that includes the

00:17:53.804 --> 00:17:53.933
more

00:17:56.339 --> 00:18:01.809
severe autism or level 3 autism— 90% have motor impairment.

00:18:02.759 --> 00:18:06.785
That's huge. And I just want to interject real quick.

00:18:07.285 --> 00:18:09.830
Can you just let our listeners know what the SPARK

00:18:09.830 --> 00:18:10.355
study was?

00:18:12.190 --> 00:18:16.143
provide some context there. Yeah, so Anandja Bhat, B-H-A-T, she

00:18:16.143 --> 00:18:16.721
was the

00:18:17.492 --> 00:18:18.344
researcher in,

00:18:19.260 --> 00:18:20.979
um, in the study. And they,

00:18:21.830 --> 00:18:23.341
they met— they measured—

00:18:24.803 --> 00:18:27.133
well, it was about 14,000

00:18:28.274 --> 00:18:33.174
participants filled out the survey for a DCD screening questionnaire.

00:18:33.270 --> 00:18:37.046
So that's— you do what— sorry, so any parent, teacher,

00:18:38.313 --> 00:18:41.342
provider can fill out the DCD questionnaire and it, and

00:18:41.422 --> 00:18:43.457
it gives you a measure of sort of risk of

00:18:43.505 --> 00:18:45.091
motor impairment. But it's, it's pretty,

00:18:45.828 --> 00:18:47.896
uh, the psychometric properties are pretty good. So if, if

00:18:47.928 --> 00:18:50.892
they're, if, if you're flagged, there's a good chance you

00:18:50.892 --> 00:18:54.305
have motor impairment. And it also, that study also showed

00:18:54.898 --> 00:18:55.635
the more severe

00:18:56.356 --> 00:19:00.747
symptoms of autism, like without being, without functional speech, call

00:19:00.747 --> 00:19:05.075
that more severe symptom, the worse your motor impairment Okay.

00:19:05.299 --> 00:19:05.556
So,

00:19:06.822 --> 00:19:10.173
um, the— and DCD in autism was

00:19:10.991 --> 00:19:13.460
added to the DSM, I think it was in 2015,

00:19:14.631 --> 00:19:15.657
so a while ago.

00:19:16.442 --> 00:19:19.601
The process to assess DCD was developed before they knew

00:19:19.745 --> 00:19:23.754
that the prevalence of motor impairment and DCD in autism

00:19:23.754 --> 00:19:24.331
was so high.

00:19:25.758 --> 00:19:26.576
But I have, um,

00:19:27.586 --> 00:19:31.434
I've administered the motor— the standardized test to people like

00:19:31.434 --> 00:19:32.946
our children. And

00:19:34.982 --> 00:19:37.275
it's really problematic because they,

00:19:38.131 --> 00:19:40.700
you find that they all fall under basically below the

00:19:40.700 --> 00:19:43.968
first percentile. So that means that these tests, yeah, are

00:19:43.968 --> 00:19:46.962
not sensitive enough to get a good baseline. The floor

00:19:47.236 --> 00:19:50.699
of the scoring system is way too high. So it's

00:19:50.699 --> 00:19:55.859
basically impossible, almost impossible to get a DCD diagnosis in

00:19:55.973 --> 00:19:57.283
nonspeaking autism.

00:19:58.408 --> 00:20:01.525
Um, but I do think it's— you don't necessarily

00:20:02.602 --> 00:20:05.751
need the diagnosis, but I do believe it's important to

00:20:05.815 --> 00:20:09.140
have the motor impairment documented so that

00:20:10.056 --> 00:20:10.763
the team knows.

00:20:12.145 --> 00:20:14.587
Um, that's what I was going to ask then. What

00:20:14.587 --> 00:20:17.447
are your options to do that? I mean, for parents,

00:20:17.463 --> 00:20:20.692
what are your options to try to screen for motor

00:20:20.692 --> 00:20:23.697
impairment or motor differences? What, you know, if they're noticing

00:20:23.873 --> 00:20:24.179
things

00:20:25.347 --> 00:20:25.928
What can they do?

00:20:26.734 --> 00:20:27.121
So

00:20:28.556 --> 00:20:30.040
this is something I'm, I'm

00:20:31.072 --> 00:20:34.942
starting to teach other physiotherapists and occupational therapists, is that,

00:20:37.021 --> 00:20:38.278
um, is that you can pull,

00:20:39.600 --> 00:20:42.810
you can pull tasks and, and tests from other standardized,

00:20:44.280 --> 00:20:47.107
um, other, other more tests of motor function. So

00:20:47.931 --> 00:20:50.499
I kind of do a, a combination myself. So right

00:20:50.499 --> 00:20:53.710
now there's no consensus. This is part of the problem

00:20:53.710 --> 00:20:56.825
is that there's no consensus on how to measure motor

00:20:56.825 --> 00:20:58.559
impairment, especially in

00:20:59.699 --> 00:21:04.163
level 3 autism. Nonspeak, like particularly nonspeaking? No, but in

00:21:04.275 --> 00:21:06.989
general what I do is I'll take the motor milestones

00:21:07.021 --> 00:21:10.088
because those are, those, um, like anyone can Google those

00:21:10.088 --> 00:21:11.613
and those would basically be

00:21:12.384 --> 00:21:12.930
the rolling,

00:21:14.230 --> 00:21:19.987
army crawling, um, Yeah, like 4-point crawling. Okay, jumping, ball

00:21:19.987 --> 00:21:20.860
handling skills,

00:21:22.993 --> 00:21:25.160
balancing on on one foot, and

00:21:26.052 --> 00:21:28.256
you know a squat sits to stand. So there's yeah

00:21:28.272 --> 00:21:30.866
there's those kind of motor milestones.

00:21:32.317 --> 00:21:33.799
I would assess those. And by the way, when you

00:21:34.508 --> 00:21:35.071
the the

00:21:36.199 --> 00:21:37.310
the funny thing is is that

00:21:38.840 --> 00:21:42.263
the this the majority of thought is. These guys don't

00:21:42.263 --> 00:21:45.836
need physical intervention or physiotherapy because we see them walking,

00:21:45.932 --> 00:21:48.336
we see them climbing stairs. Right. So there's no problem,

00:21:48.432 --> 00:21:48.737
right?

00:21:50.018 --> 00:21:52.630
Right. But when you actually go in and, and look

00:21:52.678 --> 00:21:52.887
at their

00:21:53.624 --> 00:21:56.684
motor function and go back to those motor milestones, there

00:21:56.684 --> 00:21:57.950
are so many gaps.

00:21:59.328 --> 00:22:02.373
There are like these, these skills that are foundational for

00:22:02.533 --> 00:22:05.738
brain development, um, including the higher order ones like,

00:22:06.539 --> 00:22:08.093
like speech. They're, they're missing.

00:22:09.087 --> 00:22:10.353
They're missing. And,

00:22:11.090 --> 00:22:13.767
um, so I'll find out where the gaps in their,

00:22:14.232 --> 00:22:17.614
in their motor function are. And Elizabeth Torres developed 3

00:22:17.678 --> 00:22:20.291
really simple, really great validated,

00:22:21.589 --> 00:22:23.096
uh, functional measures.

00:22:23.849 --> 00:22:26.254
2 minutes of sitting still, can they do that? 2

00:22:26.254 --> 00:22:28.899
minutes of pointing to a target, and 2 minutes of

00:22:28.899 --> 00:22:32.153
walking. So I use— I've just begun to incorporate those

00:22:32.185 --> 00:22:32.698
ones as well.

00:22:34.012 --> 00:22:36.160
That's great. I have to say though, there's

00:22:36.920 --> 00:22:39.330
Sure. It's difficult to find

00:22:40.648 --> 00:22:45.115
PTs and OTs who have experience working with

00:22:46.260 --> 00:22:47.432
nonspeaking autism to do

00:22:49.184 --> 00:22:52.493
an adequate motor function assessment. Like,

00:22:54.004 --> 00:22:57.133
it's a problem right now, even though I can't speak

00:22:57.133 --> 00:22:59.714
for the US curriculum, but the CDD in autism was

00:23:00.234 --> 00:23:03.091
introduced in the OT and PT curriculums about 8 or

00:23:03.091 --> 00:23:04.195
9 years ago in Canada.

00:23:04.973 --> 00:23:07.651
But it's only taught at a superficial level, like most

00:23:07.651 --> 00:23:11.570
specializations. But this is where we're at. There's,

00:23:12.684 --> 00:23:13.297
there's, um,

00:23:15.185 --> 00:23:17.864
there's this knowledge gap that we're trying to correct, but

00:23:18.881 --> 00:23:21.552
parents will find that it is, that it is going

00:23:21.552 --> 00:23:24.574
to be a challenge to find a practitioner who has

00:23:24.607 --> 00:23:27.856
that experience to administer the test. And not impossible, but—

00:23:29.140 --> 00:23:31.718
So in some ways it Feels a little bit like,

00:23:31.734 --> 00:23:35.100
kind of like where even autism diagnosis was 20 years

00:23:35.100 --> 00:23:38.114
ago, like how difficult it was for parents back then.

00:23:39.589 --> 00:23:41.529
I mean, I know it's still not like super easy,

00:23:41.977 --> 00:23:45.248
but it's at least easier. I mean, it's more obviously

00:23:45.264 --> 00:23:46.290
more pervasive and

00:23:47.027 --> 00:23:49.945
yeah, a little more accessible. But that was like, you

00:23:49.945 --> 00:23:53.295
know, kind of winning a lottery ticket is if you

00:23:53.295 --> 00:23:55.459
were able to get, you know, get in and be

00:23:55.459 --> 00:23:59.626
assessed within 12 months or the waiting periods are just

00:23:59.642 --> 00:24:00.254
insane.

00:24:02.381 --> 00:24:05.185
In Canada, it's, it's, it's laughable now. It's something in

00:24:05.185 --> 00:24:06.490
Ontario, it's something like,

00:24:08.476 --> 00:24:11.303
I've heard even 6, 7 years and it's 4 to

00:24:11.303 --> 00:24:14.388
5 years here in my province. Like— For a diagnosis?

00:24:15.082 --> 00:24:17.440
For the assessment. If you want it publicly funded. Oh,

00:24:17.456 --> 00:24:20.206
for the assessment. Oh, for, okay. So for the autism

00:24:20.206 --> 00:24:21.306
assessment or for— Yes.

00:24:22.050 --> 00:24:22.083
Wow.

00:24:22.843 --> 00:24:25.595
And to get start earlier intervention. Yes. Early

00:24:26.602 --> 00:24:28.897
start, early intervention. You have to wait 5 years.

00:24:30.520 --> 00:24:34.192
I know, it's laughable. I like— yeah, I know, it's

00:24:34.192 --> 00:24:36.955
awful. Wow. Yeah, that's a, that's a real, that's a

00:24:36.955 --> 00:24:39.808
real problem. You touched on this a little bit, but,

00:24:39.904 --> 00:24:41.660
um, can you talk a little bit more about what

00:24:41.660 --> 00:24:44.624
can happen over time when some of these physical needs

00:24:44.656 --> 00:24:46.847
are ignored, or some of these, you know, milestones that

00:24:46.847 --> 00:24:47.752
you had talked about?

00:24:48.756 --> 00:24:50.827
Yeah, I've, I've worked with enough

00:24:51.838 --> 00:24:54.806
enough clients now, um, at different ages that I can

00:24:54.806 --> 00:24:56.715
see that I've seen changes

00:24:57.934 --> 00:24:58.543
happen that,

00:24:59.313 --> 00:25:02.281
that make me, um, that have really made me think

00:25:02.281 --> 00:25:03.371
about what these—

00:25:04.125 --> 00:25:06.195
this population is going to be like in their 60s

00:25:06.211 --> 00:25:06.965
and 70s. And I

00:25:07.734 --> 00:25:09.707
truthfully, I lose sleep over this because,

00:25:11.087 --> 00:25:11.953
um, because

00:25:13.188 --> 00:25:15.257
the way we are, the way it is now, there's

00:25:15.257 --> 00:25:16.605
a lot of them that are not going to receive

00:25:16.621 --> 00:25:19.124
physical intervention and they're going to age this way. And

00:25:19.124 --> 00:25:20.986
of course, we know that they're at higher risk for

00:25:21.050 --> 00:25:24.806
comorbidities. They have an accelerated rate of aging because of

00:25:24.806 --> 00:25:27.888
inflammation, and they're at higher risk of Parkinson's symptoms and

00:25:27.888 --> 00:25:29.445
other comorbidities down the line.

00:25:30.424 --> 00:25:33.378
And of course, at this point, still the majority

00:25:34.437 --> 00:25:36.572
who don't have communication will not be able to tell

00:25:36.620 --> 00:25:39.285
someone when they're in pain. So I, I think about

00:25:39.285 --> 00:25:39.991
this all the time.

00:25:40.938 --> 00:25:41.082
And,

00:25:41.821 --> 00:25:44.341
and I have seen even some individuals in their 20s

00:25:44.341 --> 00:25:47.467
who have some postural changes already. Often from— oh yeah,

00:25:47.484 --> 00:25:49.619
you had mentioned that. I remember you talking about that.

00:25:50.137 --> 00:25:53.130
Often from stimming and sitting in a poor position. But

00:25:53.130 --> 00:25:57.139
I've even seen, um, children who are just— who

00:25:58.156 --> 00:26:00.464
can't sit to stand without using their hands, which is

00:26:00.496 --> 00:26:02.110
kind of like what, what elderly people do.

00:26:02.821 --> 00:26:04.727
And I see a lot who have core weakness and

00:26:04.759 --> 00:26:08.717
especially upper body weakness just from lack of activity overhead.

00:26:10.421 --> 00:26:10.573
And

00:26:11.450 --> 00:26:13.656
what I know is that So I don't know if

00:26:13.656 --> 00:26:16.209
you've ever had a sort of an overuse injury or

00:26:16.209 --> 00:26:18.842
an imbalance that you've had to correct. It's not easy.

00:26:19.275 --> 00:26:21.764
It takes like kind of dedicated—

00:26:22.952 --> 00:26:23.289
You're right.

00:26:23.996 --> 00:26:26.436
It takes time and attention. You have to sort of

00:26:26.806 --> 00:26:28.797
like put in the work to correct something.

00:26:29.712 --> 00:26:32.907
And often that takes the skill of isolating a muscle

00:26:32.907 --> 00:26:36.118
and, or maybe relaxing into a stretch. 2 things that

00:26:36.118 --> 00:26:37.933
are very, very difficult for

00:26:38.874 --> 00:26:41.818
People with significant motor impairment and autism. So

00:26:43.121 --> 00:26:47.077
once you have one of those problems begin, now you're

00:26:47.077 --> 00:26:49.830
really facing a mountain now to try and correct. And

00:26:49.830 --> 00:26:50.517
that's not impossible,

00:26:51.610 --> 00:26:54.831
but prevention of these, of loss of range of motion,

00:26:55.008 --> 00:26:56.039
of weakness,

00:26:58.246 --> 00:27:02.539
of pain, it's really crucial to have. And we know

00:27:02.621 --> 00:27:04.643
that, we know that movement and exercise, that's one of

00:27:04.643 --> 00:27:07.473
the One of the things we can hopefully control in

00:27:07.473 --> 00:27:10.315
our lives and, and, and do for ourselves to help,

00:27:10.475 --> 00:27:11.711
um, age healthy.

00:27:12.755 --> 00:27:14.891
So these— But also, does it— does also that they're

00:27:14.907 --> 00:27:18.214
also the argument, or just, you know, that's sort of

00:27:18.214 --> 00:27:20.864
the argument, but just the point that it can help

00:27:20.864 --> 00:27:23.417
with regulation. It could help in other areas too.

00:27:24.460 --> 00:27:26.837
That's excellent point. It can also help with regulation,

00:27:27.897 --> 00:27:28.121
um,

00:27:29.037 --> 00:27:29.245
which

00:27:30.080 --> 00:27:30.257
just

00:27:31.037 --> 00:27:33.719
Helps with being able to participate in life more.

00:27:35.910 --> 00:27:38.416
And, and for if they have access to communication.

00:27:39.176 --> 00:27:43.219
Yeah. And self-esteem, of course, when you feel more competent

