I mean, such a huge challenge.

00:30:39.512 --> 00:30:40.682
Yeah, to be able to have that,

00:30:41.756 --> 00:30:45.331
that vision assessment by an optometrist and for my son

00:30:45.331 --> 00:30:47.223
to be able to answer questions, that was just,

00:30:48.249 --> 00:30:48.441
um,

00:30:49.771 --> 00:30:50.284
yeah, like

00:30:51.294 --> 00:30:53.955
incredibly helpful because, yeah, my son wears glasses when he

00:30:54.100 --> 00:30:57.338
uses a letterboard. His glasses actually have a, um, a

00:30:57.402 --> 00:31:00.832
tint to them. He's a bit light sensitive, so, and

00:31:00.832 --> 00:31:03.687
he wears prism glasses to help In theory, correct his

00:31:03.687 --> 00:31:06.544
center of gravity, his visual processing for when we're doing

00:31:06.544 --> 00:31:07.523
gross motor skills. So

00:31:08.791 --> 00:31:12.932
yeah, the eyes are like— people forget about how, um,

00:31:13.991 --> 00:31:15.019
how important they are. And

00:31:16.511 --> 00:31:18.758
to— And then again, if you can't communicate, like my,

00:31:18.806 --> 00:31:22.482
my, my eyes/glasses story with Stone was, you know, from

00:31:22.530 --> 00:31:23.734
a few, a few years ago,

00:31:24.601 --> 00:31:26.912
not long after he was able to communicate. We were

00:31:26.928 --> 00:31:29.031
able to figure out somehow— I was able to figure

00:31:29.031 --> 00:31:31.791
out he needed glasses, but even getting the right prescription,

00:31:31.823 --> 00:31:33.701
then we finally got it and we went down, you

00:31:33.701 --> 00:31:34.279
know, we live in

00:31:35.033 --> 00:31:37.567
Yeah, Edmonds, Washington, which is right on the Puget Sound.

00:31:37.759 --> 00:31:39.587
We have a beautiful— we have a nice beach that

00:31:39.587 --> 00:31:41.849
we've spent a lot of time at. And on a

00:31:41.849 --> 00:31:44.094
clear day, you can actually look out to the west

00:31:44.158 --> 00:31:46.323
and see the Olympic Mountain Range. It's

00:31:47.221 --> 00:31:50.589
beautiful, you know, the snow-capped mountains, especially in the winter.

00:31:51.936 --> 00:31:54.502
But Stone told me, uh, again, this was like so

00:31:54.502 --> 00:31:56.523
powerful a couple years ago. He said, you know, this

00:31:56.523 --> 00:31:58.864
is the first time I've been able to see the

00:31:58.864 --> 00:32:00.179
Olympic Range.

00:32:00.933 --> 00:32:02.779
Wow. Because he was wearing his glasses. He's like, yeah,

00:32:02.795 --> 00:32:04.545
I'd never seen that. He kind of knew they were

00:32:04.545 --> 00:32:06.407
there, but had never been able to see it before.

00:32:06.712 --> 00:32:08.622
I mean, that's one of those little things that just

00:32:08.622 --> 00:32:11.865
means, means so much. Yeah. I generally always recommend to

00:32:11.865 --> 00:32:13.904
my parents, if they can, if they have a good

00:32:13.936 --> 00:32:18.318
developmental optometrist, that's really great to bring in as part

00:32:18.334 --> 00:32:18.607
of the

00:32:20.020 --> 00:32:21.336
assessment to help them.

00:32:22.684 --> 00:32:23.664
Yeah. And so

00:32:24.531 --> 00:32:27.308
with pointing, I mean, of course you need shoulder stability

00:32:27.324 --> 00:32:29.554
and you need You need the right level of muscle

00:32:29.586 --> 00:32:31.335
tone and you need, and you need to be able

00:32:31.335 --> 00:32:31.800
to gauge

00:32:32.634 --> 00:32:34.126
the force of, of,

00:32:34.944 --> 00:32:35.217
um,

00:32:36.388 --> 00:32:39.019
and the depth perception because the, the movement goes like

00:32:39.019 --> 00:32:42.725
there's an acceleration and there's a deceleration and then you

00:32:42.725 --> 00:32:45.099
have to do the same thing in reverse. By the

00:32:45.147 --> 00:32:48.307
way, reversal of direction is like universally hard for this

00:32:48.356 --> 00:32:51.933
population, whether it's pointing or— Oh, interesting. Moving their body,

00:32:52.783 --> 00:32:55.305
like without practice, it's, it's quite hard. And then you

00:32:55.337 --> 00:32:56.239
have to do that process,

00:32:57.011 --> 00:32:59.603
um, like letter by letter. So it's,

00:33:00.424 --> 00:33:04.517
it's an extraordinarily complex and difficult motor task. It's not,

00:33:04.988 --> 00:33:07.670
um, like some calling it just pointing is really an

00:33:07.670 --> 00:33:09.555
injustice to how hard they have to work to,

00:33:11.182 --> 00:33:12.878
to gain access to communication that way.

00:33:14.186 --> 00:33:20.651
Which underscores again, um, kind of the whole test Testing

00:33:21.629 --> 00:33:26.362
aside, when you hear skeptics talk about just, oh, well,

00:33:26.443 --> 00:33:30.406
just put the letterboard on a stand and they have

00:33:30.406 --> 00:33:34.481
a point to it that way, and they'll determine, they'll

00:33:34.481 --> 00:33:35.733
show if they can actually do it.

00:33:36.888 --> 00:33:39.631
I know that's bogus, right? I mean, because it's just,

00:33:39.696 --> 00:33:40.594
it's not that simple.

00:33:41.460 --> 00:33:45.295
No, it's not. It's not. That's why it's so crucial

00:33:45.295 --> 00:33:48.488
to understand the level of challenge and the pathophysiology.

00:33:50.027 --> 00:33:52.528
As a practitioner, if you don't have that knowledge, you're

00:33:52.528 --> 00:33:56.263
really flying blind working with this population. It's pretty dangerous.

00:33:56.856 --> 00:33:57.946
And then with the pointing,

00:33:58.844 --> 00:34:02.419
we have these quote unquote tests of not, um, nonverbal

00:34:02.643 --> 00:34:03.252
tests of IQ.

00:34:03.990 --> 00:34:07.548
Of course, we hate saying that nonverbal because that translates

00:34:07.917 --> 00:34:10.017
directly from Latin to without words, and our kids are

00:34:10.017 --> 00:34:13.463
not without words. Right, right, right. But people have designed

00:34:13.463 --> 00:34:13.656
these

00:34:14.569 --> 00:34:18.148
tests, um, To test cognition and understanding,

00:34:19.385 --> 00:34:22.999
but things still rely on pointing and eye gaze. So

00:34:24.027 --> 00:34:27.432
if you're— so if you're using— if there's no reliable

00:34:27.432 --> 00:34:30.114
means of communication and you're using this test on someone,

00:34:30.210 --> 00:34:31.527
you're asking them to point to,

00:34:32.700 --> 00:34:35.527
uh, a picture or a word or something. And like,

00:34:35.591 --> 00:34:38.193
what are you— are you measuring language and understanding, or

00:34:38.257 --> 00:34:39.494
are you measuring someone's

00:34:40.586 --> 00:34:41.373
motor— Good point.

00:34:42.257 --> 00:34:45.434
their motor skill in a very challenging situation in, in

00:34:45.482 --> 00:34:48.098
that moment. If they're not valid, so if there is

00:34:48.130 --> 00:34:49.863
no reliable means of communication,

00:34:51.195 --> 00:34:54.694
it's untestable. You should not be, you should not be

00:34:54.774 --> 00:34:56.876
using that test. And we know this from the CP

00:34:56.876 --> 00:34:58.754
popu— from the cerebral palsy population,

00:34:59.604 --> 00:35:03.054
that these tests are invalid in populations with known motor

00:35:03.054 --> 00:35:03.536
impairment.

00:35:04.322 --> 00:35:06.216
You shouldn't be using them. Interesting. Talk, talk about that

00:35:06.264 --> 00:35:07.949
a little bit more, cuz again, I don't, I don't

00:35:07.949 --> 00:35:08.655
know how many people

00:35:09.377 --> 00:35:12.491
Realize that. Right. So, so tests of

00:35:13.808 --> 00:35:15.477
cognition and intellectual

00:35:16.280 --> 00:35:20.871
ability, they're used for educational placements. Yeah. And they're used

00:35:20.871 --> 00:35:22.204
for funding decisions.

00:35:23.584 --> 00:35:27.790
And this is, this is really serious because you're the—

00:35:28.031 --> 00:35:32.927
many educational psychologists are still using these to determine, like,

00:35:33.939 --> 00:35:37.406
major life things that really set a person's trajectory on,

00:35:37.631 --> 00:35:38.819
on life and, and their family.

00:35:40.269 --> 00:35:43.097
And they shouldn't be unless there is a reliable means

00:35:43.258 --> 00:35:44.115
of communication.

00:35:45.278 --> 00:35:48.881
Cognition and intelligence, they're untestable. You shouldn't be testing.

00:35:48.978 --> 00:35:52.375
You're, you're, you're, it's a garbage result that you're, you're

00:35:52.375 --> 00:35:52.763
using.

00:35:54.150 --> 00:35:55.610
Mm-hmm. And so if, if you,

00:35:57.470 --> 00:36:00.245
if you can't test, then the, the safest assumption, the

00:36:00.245 --> 00:36:03.235
most logical assumption based on the,

00:36:04.130 --> 00:36:06.569
the knowledge that we have in the research and understand

00:36:06.569 --> 00:36:09.040
the physiology is that there's no reason to believe

00:36:09.782 --> 00:36:10.520
That, um,

00:36:11.546 --> 00:36:12.556
the lack of speech

00:36:13.935 --> 00:36:17.285
has anything to do with the lack of intelligence. So

00:36:17.301 --> 00:36:17.878
you should keep

00:36:18.744 --> 00:36:21.854
progressing, keep going on that track until you are proven

00:36:21.886 --> 00:36:25.333
otherwise. That's of course the least dangerous assumption. And the

00:36:25.333 --> 00:36:28.234
least dangerous assumption, exactly, which is, um,

00:36:29.501 --> 00:36:31.617
I mean, it makes all the sense in the world

00:36:32.066 --> 00:36:34.903
when you really understand it, but it's, it's

00:36:35.993 --> 00:36:40.223
Not practiced very often at all, unfortunately. I mean, maybe

00:36:40.223 --> 00:36:41.153
we're seeing it

00:36:42.082 --> 00:36:44.582
gaining a little bit more traction, but it feels like

00:36:44.582 --> 00:36:47.995
it's just such a fight to, to get there, to

00:36:47.995 --> 00:36:50.896
get that. It is. It's only the not— like, only

00:36:50.896 --> 00:36:53.588
the nonspeaking population, of course, not just autism, but with

00:36:53.684 --> 00:36:57.242
Rett syndrome and Down syndrome, they're expected to prove their,

00:36:58.267 --> 00:37:02.776
their intelligence, prove their capacity Their capacity. They're learning before

00:37:03.001 --> 00:37:06.345
they are given the right to learn. And whether that's

00:37:07.245 --> 00:37:08.113
learning physically,

00:37:09.094 --> 00:37:10.127
learning intellectually,

00:37:10.951 --> 00:37:12.228
it's, uh, it's really,

00:37:14.765 --> 00:37:15.734
it's really tragic.

00:37:16.704 --> 00:37:18.267
It is. And dehumanizing.

00:37:19.263 --> 00:37:19.521
Yeah.

00:37:20.308 --> 00:37:23.699
And, and you think about the damage that's done, not

00:37:23.699 --> 00:37:26.319
only obviously the lost opportunity, which is

00:37:27.384 --> 00:37:30.100
So obvious. I mean, that's horrible in and of itself,

00:37:30.662 --> 00:37:32.286
but the trauma that goes along with it,

00:37:34.214 --> 00:37:36.805
you know, the trauma that goes along with knowing that

00:37:37.337 --> 00:37:39.579
you're being seen this way as maybe not worth.

00:37:40.885 --> 00:37:43.337
And I find when I work with parents,

00:37:44.162 --> 00:37:46.576
uh, because of course I never have just one client,

00:37:46.657 --> 00:37:49.233
I have the parent and I have the child, and

00:37:49.654 --> 00:37:54.804
both need, um, both need support and empowering. And I

00:37:54.804 --> 00:37:58.299
know this because I've, I've had experience of being disempowered

00:37:58.332 --> 00:38:01.231
by my own fellow rehab med practitioners. So I take

00:38:01.231 --> 00:38:04.949
that role very seriously of supporting and encouraging the, the

00:38:04.949 --> 00:38:07.310
parents because they don't, um,

00:38:08.894 --> 00:38:10.900
they don't need my degrees to be able to be

00:38:10.900 --> 00:38:14.957
a great support person and, and therapist their, their child.

00:38:15.038 --> 00:38:17.388
It's— no, it's not easy, but like

00:38:18.409 --> 00:38:18.506
Like,

00:38:19.555 --> 00:38:21.816
you can do this, right? That's, that's the message I

00:38:21.816 --> 00:38:24.691
want to send. I never want to make someone dependent

00:38:24.788 --> 00:38:27.982
on my service to help them. It's almost— it's also,

00:38:28.063 --> 00:38:29.264
I would say, even unethical,

00:38:30.092 --> 00:38:31.958
just given that they will need support for— Right.

00:38:34.339 --> 00:38:36.182
Uh, yeah, for, you know, long term.

00:38:36.975 --> 00:38:37.233
Um,

00:38:38.689 --> 00:38:40.096
I was gonna say, the longer,

00:38:40.937 --> 00:38:42.538
the longer a parent

00:38:43.281 --> 00:38:44.990
goes with that mindset of

00:38:46.958 --> 00:38:49.176
My child can't do this. They can't do that. This

00:38:49.240 --> 00:38:50.795
is— they're not going to be able to do this.

00:38:50.908 --> 00:38:51.475
They don't understand.

00:38:52.616 --> 00:38:53.163
It's so

00:38:54.079 --> 00:38:57.776
difficult to then— I— for me to come in and

00:38:57.841 --> 00:38:58.178
say,

00:38:59.448 --> 00:39:02.523
no, what you were told was wrong. And

00:39:03.716 --> 00:39:05.877
this is like, this is their potential.

00:39:06.780 --> 00:39:08.731
And to, to change that mindset,

00:39:10.196 --> 00:39:12.245
it can be very difficult. You have to come at

00:39:12.245 --> 00:39:13.739
it very sensitively and,

00:39:14.799 --> 00:39:15.426
and patiently.

00:39:16.744 --> 00:39:17.145
But it's— Yeah.

00:39:17.949 --> 00:39:20.166
But there's a lot of other practitioners out there making

00:39:20.198 --> 00:39:24.761
my job much harder because they've set the family on

00:39:24.761 --> 00:39:25.597
this trajectory,

00:39:27.091 --> 00:39:30.722
and they— which sets the bar very low, and they

00:39:30.722 --> 00:39:30.931
lose—

00:39:31.686 --> 00:39:32.458
the child loses

00:39:33.357 --> 00:39:35.751
much opportunity to fulfill their potential

00:39:36.828 --> 00:39:40.422
and quality of life. Right. But the upside is we've

00:39:40.422 --> 00:39:43.372
seen example after example of

00:39:44.206 --> 00:39:47.317
children or young adults or even adults, once given this

00:39:47.317 --> 00:39:48.023
opportunity,

00:39:49.322 --> 00:39:52.914
you know, take it and really start to flourish in

00:39:53.010 --> 00:39:56.121
ways they're so capable. Yeah. When you give— when you

00:39:56.137 --> 00:39:59.874
have high expectations and high effective support, my own son

00:39:59.922 --> 00:40:03.193
has like blown my own expectations of what he can

00:40:03.193 --> 00:40:07.105
learn physically. out of the water and continues to do

00:40:07.169 --> 00:40:07.443
so.

