twice a couple years. And I remember at the time,

00:40:41.300 --> 00:40:44.214
again, my mindset was he's afraid to do it because

00:40:44.214 --> 00:40:46.227
this is just how we're programmed, right? Like, he got

00:40:46.227 --> 00:40:47.773
to the end edge of the— you know, and that's

00:40:47.773 --> 00:40:51.175
normal. Like, again, lots of kids are scared to jump

00:40:51.223 --> 00:40:53.525
off the diving board, so there's nothing wrong with that.

00:40:53.606 --> 00:40:55.098
So I just always thought it was just kind of

00:40:55.098 --> 00:40:56.817
a fear factor sort of a thing, that he was

00:40:56.817 --> 00:40:58.178
afraid to just take that jump.

00:40:59.154 --> 00:41:01.290
Not understanding previously

00:41:02.510 --> 00:41:05.400
the impact of apraxia, right? And it was literally like

00:41:05.416 --> 00:41:07.985
about a year ago. It's funny because we, for whatever

00:41:07.985 --> 00:41:09.848
reason, we didn't happen to go to the pool a

00:41:09.848 --> 00:41:11.598
lot last year, so we didn't get a chance to

00:41:12.320 --> 00:41:14.006
test the diving board. But I was like, you know

00:41:14.151 --> 00:41:17.458
what, because I was seeing so much improvement with him

00:41:17.458 --> 00:41:19.947
with his apraxia and his, his motor control and all

00:41:19.947 --> 00:41:21.617
that, I'm like, I bet he'll, I bet he'll do

00:41:21.633 --> 00:41:22.773
it. Like, you know, this is going to be a

00:41:22.773 --> 00:41:24.009
lot easier for him to do it.

00:41:24.828 --> 00:41:27.193
So I wasn't surprised. I was thrilled, of course, that

00:41:27.193 --> 00:41:29.172
he was able to, you know, they, like he said,

00:41:29.188 --> 00:41:31.408
he did it just again and again and again.

00:41:32.289 --> 00:41:33.194
That was the cool part.

00:41:34.729 --> 00:41:37.072
I should too just mention then, 'cause you used the

00:41:37.072 --> 00:41:38.688
word apraxia and that's,

00:41:39.722 --> 00:41:42.768
that's considered like we're talking about the same thing, the

00:41:42.784 --> 00:41:46.814
DCD apraxia, dyspraxia. That's like a dyspraxia comes from UK

00:41:46.814 --> 00:41:48.127
term. And so there's,

00:41:49.432 --> 00:41:49.699
um,

00:41:50.970 --> 00:41:52.833
Yeah, that's actually part of the problem is that they're

00:41:52.833 --> 00:41:55.451
like, people are using the, the terms interchangeably

00:41:56.190 --> 00:42:00.543
and that causes confusion, I think, to, to get the

00:42:00.704 --> 00:42:01.507
motor impairment,

00:42:02.374 --> 00:42:02.583
um,

00:42:03.515 --> 00:42:06.535
acknowledged and documented. Not that there's anything, I'm not saying

00:42:06.567 --> 00:42:09.715
there's anything wrong with using the word, um, apraxia, but—

00:42:09.715 --> 00:42:12.108
Correct. But I, yeah, but I, it, it is the

00:42:12.108 --> 00:42:15.739
fact that we have these different terminologies being used. Mm-hmm.

00:42:15.884 --> 00:42:17.350
Is definitely contributing to

00:42:18.542 --> 00:42:21.120
like a lack of awareness of the, the motor impairment.

00:42:21.764 --> 00:42:22.553
So I hope,

00:42:24.283 --> 00:42:26.263
like I said, I would like to see just, um,

00:42:28.050 --> 00:42:31.592
it not be a dual diagnosis, just the motor impairment.

00:42:32.252 --> 00:42:37.179
Motor impairment. Yeah. Yeah. Apraxia specifically refers to mostly the

00:42:37.422 --> 00:42:40.172
motor planning part, which is like a very significant part

00:42:40.172 --> 00:42:40.819
of the motor dysfunction.

00:42:44.637 --> 00:42:47.870
Um, which, yeah, which people should, should be aware of

00:42:47.870 --> 00:42:49.468
when they're working with these guys.

00:42:50.711 --> 00:42:53.874
Well, yeah, and that makes sense. Maybe there is kind

00:42:53.874 --> 00:42:56.052
of a part A, part B sort of a thing.

00:42:56.100 --> 00:42:58.181
Like you said, there's just kind of the motor impairment

00:42:58.843 --> 00:43:00.165
and then there's the motor planning.

00:43:00.940 --> 00:43:03.358
And all this is just, um, you know, it's just

00:43:03.358 --> 00:43:05.899
a different language if you're a parent. Yeah, right, right.

00:43:05.947 --> 00:43:07.571
I mean, like, you have to learn it. It took

00:43:07.636 --> 00:43:10.080
me a long time to really understand it and understand

00:43:10.112 --> 00:43:10.659
the concept.

00:43:11.587 --> 00:43:14.106
Motor planning. What does that mean? Like, what, you know,

00:43:14.170 --> 00:43:14.507
like,

00:43:15.453 --> 00:43:18.790
until you see it in practice and understand it, oh,

00:43:18.822 --> 00:43:21.517
it really means like planning and being able to, to

00:43:21.549 --> 00:43:23.731
do things. You know, the example I always use, you

00:43:24.180 --> 00:43:26.747
ask them to go find something and they're able to

00:43:26.747 --> 00:43:27.373
do it. Right.

00:43:28.127 --> 00:43:29.619
Do things in sequence and,

00:43:30.357 --> 00:43:32.570
um, with the right steps. And

00:43:33.581 --> 00:43:33.693
yeah,

00:43:34.848 --> 00:43:36.453
all those things that seem simple. All right. Well, I

00:43:36.453 --> 00:43:39.390
want to talk about, um, Getting back into kind of

00:43:39.390 --> 00:43:43.100
the physiotherapy, you, you have a couple of unique areas

