00:46:06.161 --> 00:46:07.831
getting his heart rate up. And, and

00:46:08.601 --> 00:46:10.671
so that's been fun too, to sort of mix in

00:46:10.671 --> 00:46:11.281
those workouts.

00:46:12.116 --> 00:46:13.721
Do you, do you have those on video? Are you

00:46:13.721 --> 00:46:17.530
gonna I should. I'll just put them on video. Yeah.

00:46:17.530 --> 00:46:19.750
Because seriously, I mean, you know, those are popular. There's

00:46:19.750 --> 00:46:22.290
so many parents that just don't know what to do.

00:46:22.290 --> 00:46:24.200
I know. And they know they need to do something.

00:46:24.370 --> 00:46:26.490
They know, right? But you need ideas. And I don't—

00:46:26.490 --> 00:46:29.030
I needed them too. Like, you, yeah, you really do

00:46:29.030 --> 00:46:32.370
need ideas because, and once you, once, once sort of

00:46:32.370 --> 00:46:35.070
like you, you get examples of like, oh, you can,

00:46:35.070 --> 00:46:36.930
you can do this with them and you can do

00:46:36.930 --> 00:46:39.050
this and it really opens up your world and, and

00:46:39.050 --> 00:46:41.947
then you that confidence in problem solving. Because I still

00:46:42.043 --> 00:46:44.194
have to problem solve through when I'm coaching something.

00:46:45.013 --> 00:46:47.677
There's, there's still lots of times where I have to

00:46:47.677 --> 00:46:49.844
think, okay, how can I digress this? And, or how,

00:46:50.100 --> 00:46:51.416
how can I, um,

00:46:52.813 --> 00:46:55.718
what's the component that he's working on? And, and, but

00:46:56.023 --> 00:46:58.286
being comfortable with that trial and error and that problem

00:46:58.286 --> 00:47:00.533
solving, like, that's okay. Because I, I still do a

00:47:00.533 --> 00:47:01.448
lot of sort of

00:47:02.507 --> 00:47:05.139
thinking and problem solving, especially with the, the sport skills.

00:47:06.295 --> 00:47:10.069
But because that's because you're like smart and qualified and

00:47:10.069 --> 00:47:11.979
you think, and you think that way. Most of us

00:47:11.979 --> 00:47:13.649
parents were just like, what the heck do we do?

00:47:13.922 --> 00:47:15.945
No, I can, I still, I'd reach out to lots

00:47:15.945 --> 00:47:18.402
of people to, uh, to, um,

00:47:19.285 --> 00:47:21.421
to get their, get their opinion, their eyes when I'm

00:47:22.207 --> 00:47:24.038
stuck on problems long, or I'll reach out to a

00:47:24.038 --> 00:47:24.793
self-advocate.

00:47:26.109 --> 00:47:27.538
That's, I, I think one of,

00:47:28.839 --> 00:47:29.738
if for

00:47:31.022 --> 00:47:33.945
I have this vision of how people will be educated

00:47:34.137 --> 00:47:38.890
and well-trained to work with nonspeaking autism is that there'll

00:47:38.890 --> 00:47:40.785
be a, like a, there would be a network of

00:47:40.817 --> 00:47:45.441
mentors, mentors for— Yeah. Like parent-to-parent, practitioner-to-practitioner,

00:47:45.473 --> 00:47:48.749
but practitioner-to-self-advocate and self-advocate-to-self-advocate.

00:47:49.488 --> 00:47:53.903
My best learning comes from kind of discussing challenging cases

00:47:54.610 --> 00:47:54.674
and,

00:47:55.653 --> 00:47:55.958
and

00:47:57.170 --> 00:47:57.557
And

00:47:58.718 --> 00:48:00.057
learning from self-advocates

00:48:00.961 --> 00:48:05.227
that I continually learn from that. And, and

00:48:06.942 --> 00:48:07.589
really, I, I,

00:48:08.333 --> 00:48:11.811
there, I, I get frustrated with the clinicians that,

00:48:14.029 --> 00:48:16.852
that they feel that they're well-educated and well-trained based on

00:48:16.868 --> 00:48:18.993
what they learned in school. Mm-hmm. When they don't have

00:48:18.993 --> 00:48:22.243
experience learning from either people Uh,

00:48:23.670 --> 00:48:26.718
teachers and, and mentors who have years-long experience working with

00:48:26.734 --> 00:48:30.424
communicative nonspeakers or working with communicative nonspeakers yourself.

00:48:31.788 --> 00:48:33.745
Because I can't tell you how many times I've been

00:48:34.756 --> 00:48:37.932
corrected or I've been, I've given feedback on what I'm

00:48:38.044 --> 00:48:39.905
doing and, and, and learned that

00:48:40.627 --> 00:48:43.098
something could be better or something was not helpful.

00:48:44.301 --> 00:48:47.525
You can't replace that hands-on learning with a book. Oh,

00:48:47.589 --> 00:48:48.167
no. And

00:48:49.090 --> 00:48:49.960
In a classroom.

00:48:51.621 --> 00:48:55.119
So, and I can't even imagine, again, given what's been

00:48:55.231 --> 00:48:55.892
learned

00:48:56.763 --> 00:48:59.796
just through experience and everything else, just the body of

00:48:59.893 --> 00:49:01.137
work over the last

00:49:02.089 --> 00:49:03.187
5 to 10 years,

00:49:04.813 --> 00:49:07.296
I can't imagine that academics has kept up with that

00:49:07.328 --> 00:49:09.941
very well, you know, when you're talking about, right? I

00:49:10.005 --> 00:49:12.991
mean, how, how could it even, even in, in that

00:49:12.991 --> 00:49:13.218
case?

00:49:13.986 --> 00:49:16.263
So they're already kind of coming at it kind of

00:49:16.728 --> 00:49:18.957
behind the science, I think, in a lot of the

00:49:18.957 --> 00:49:21.956
ways, even though that's true, they think differently.

00:49:23.447 --> 00:49:26.879
There in Canada, we have the National Autism Network, and

