I could only wish that we had that here. Unfortunately,

00:49:59.408 --> 00:50:02.330
not, um, not a great place for that right now,

00:50:03.647 --> 00:50:05.638
um, when it comes to kind of some of that,

00:50:05.734 --> 00:50:10.005
that high-level, um, champion and, and vision For, for our

00:50:10.005 --> 00:50:11.241
community. That's one of my,

00:50:12.333 --> 00:50:14.388
one of my big complaints. Um,

00:50:15.384 --> 00:50:18.033
but let's move on to a couple segments. Let's, let's

00:50:18.033 --> 00:50:20.361
lighten it up a little bit. And we'll start with,

00:50:20.586 --> 00:50:22.288
um, we'll start with our tip of the week, our

00:50:22.288 --> 00:50:23.701
autism tip of the week. And we'll—

00:50:24.440 --> 00:50:27.410
Hey parents, gather around, it's time to speak.

00:50:28.695 --> 00:50:33.110
We're here with your tip of the week, strategies and

00:50:33.110 --> 00:50:34.379
wisdom just for you.

00:50:35.599 --> 00:50:38.056
Autism parents, we know what you go through.

00:50:39.469 --> 00:50:43.402
All right, it's time for our tip of the week.

00:50:43.868 --> 00:50:46.662
And, um, Antigone, what do you have for us?

00:50:47.577 --> 00:50:48.476
Tip of the week. Okay,

00:50:49.327 --> 00:50:52.635
I thought of 2, and I would say my tip

00:50:52.635 --> 00:50:56.007
of the week, especially when you're working with someone to

00:50:56.007 --> 00:50:58.094
teach them a new motor skill, or even if it's

00:50:58.094 --> 00:50:59.347
an activity of daily living,

00:51:00.182 --> 00:51:02.268
My tip would be to give more breaks than you

00:51:02.268 --> 00:51:05.878
think they need. Sometimes it's not always obvious how hard

00:51:05.878 --> 00:51:06.328
they're working,

00:51:07.226 --> 00:51:10.500
but I've learned through mistake and through feedback from

00:51:11.415 --> 00:51:12.490
spellers that,

00:51:13.565 --> 00:51:13.838
um,

00:51:15.266 --> 00:51:18.090
that often, yeah, they need, they need more breaks, especially

00:51:18.155 --> 00:51:20.530
to maintain their regulation. Because I have accidentally

00:51:21.444 --> 00:51:24.493
pushed someone into dysregulation because I didn't give them enough

00:51:24.574 --> 00:51:26.708
break. And I know as a practitioner, if I'm working

00:51:26.724 --> 00:51:28.457
with someone who can't tell me when they need a

00:51:28.505 --> 00:51:31.698
break, or, um, or a beginner, I'll set a timer

00:51:31.794 --> 00:51:34.041
even for if it's 5 minutes or 10 minutes before

00:51:34.041 --> 00:51:36.897
I give them a minute or 2 break, uh, because

00:51:36.929 --> 00:51:38.197
it's just so easy to forget

00:51:38.919 --> 00:51:41.310
sometimes. And then the other tip was,

00:51:42.224 --> 00:51:44.118
again, if you're teaching a new motor skill,

00:51:45.016 --> 00:51:47.840
always do the skill yourself, even if you think you—

00:51:48.001 --> 00:51:50.054
if it's really simple. Like, um,

00:51:51.113 --> 00:51:53.424
that's good. Like the other, the other day I was,

00:51:53.921 --> 00:51:55.734
uh, one of my clients wanted to learn how to

00:51:55.878 --> 00:51:58.667
swing, um, on a swing, like pump his legs by

00:51:58.667 --> 00:51:59.196
himself.

00:52:00.013 --> 00:52:01.760
And I was teaching it, but then,

00:52:02.577 --> 00:52:02.721
um,

00:52:03.458 --> 00:52:03.763
I just,

00:52:04.900 --> 00:52:06.551
I didn't do it. I, I didn't do it at

00:52:06.599 --> 00:52:08.843
first, but then I just thought to check and I

00:52:08.907 --> 00:52:10.605
went to do it myself and really think about like,

00:52:10.685 --> 00:52:12.657
what is my body doing? What are my muscles doing?

00:52:13.009 --> 00:52:16.070
And I realized I sort of missed a component, um,

00:52:16.294 --> 00:52:19.355
in that task breakdown. So that would be other tip

00:52:19.403 --> 00:52:23.026
is like just Do yourself, even if it's something really

00:52:23.026 --> 00:52:25.638
simple and you think you know what steps are involved,

00:52:26.296 --> 00:52:28.283
just bring awareness. So you, so you then you, you

00:52:28.316 --> 00:52:30.848
put yourself in the other person's shoes of like what

00:52:30.945 --> 00:52:31.618
they have to do.

00:52:32.387 --> 00:52:35.064
Um, yeah, yeah. Again, it's not natural. I mean, like,

00:52:35.112 --> 00:52:36.956
you don't just do it naturally. You actually have to

00:52:37.036 --> 00:52:39.296
think about the steps involved. Yeah, they have to think

00:52:39.296 --> 00:52:39.793
about it. Yeah.

00:52:41.124 --> 00:52:43.400
That's great. Those are both great tips. And especially I

00:52:43.400 --> 00:52:46.109
like the breaks because I think that's easy to forget.

00:52:46.334 --> 00:52:50.811
I think, um, Again, depending on your personality or mindset,

